<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Paris (1815)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1815">1815</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-seventh-coalition">War of the Seventh Coalition</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1814">Treaty of Paris</a> of November 1815 was signed after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a> and his Hundred Days. It reduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to its 1790 borders, imposed an indemnity and an allied army of occupation. A convention signed alongside it placed the Ionian Islands under British protection as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> of the Ionian Islands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1815.jpg" alt="Treaty of Paris (1815)" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The Great Mule of Eupatoria · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coalition_occupation_of_France_1815.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> escaped from exile on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elba">Elba</a> in February 1815, entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 20 March and began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days</a> of his restored rule. After the defeat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> by the Seventh Coalition at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Battle of Waterloo</a> he abdicated again on 22 June. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xviii">Louis XVIII</a>, who had fled when Napoleon arrived, took the throne for a second time on 8 July.</p><p>The first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1814">Treaty of Paris</a>, of 30 May 1814, had been negotiated after two months. The second took five months of heated and bitter discussion, dominated by the British and the Prussians, the victors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-waterloo">Waterloo</a>. The Prussians, led by the general staff and supported by Marshal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gebhard-leberecht-von-blucher">Blücher</a>, demanded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saarland">Saarland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxembourg">Luxembourg</a> and Savoy and a far larger indemnity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>Four identical treaties were signed on 20 November 1815, between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and each of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, written in French. Castlereagh, whose concern was not to alienate the French from the restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a>, helped keep them only moderately harsher than that of 1814.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> lost the gains of the Revolutionary armies of 1790 to 1792 and was reduced to its 1790 boundaries, keeping the Comtat Venaissin, the County of Montbéliard and the Republic of Mulhouse but losing the former duchy of Bouillon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landau">Landau</a> and Saarlouis. Six communes of the Pays de Gex went to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>. France was to pay 700 million francs in five yearly instalments and to maintain a Coalition army of 150,000 men on its eastern frontier for up to five years.</p><p>Further conventions covered the indemnity, the military line, private claims and the claims of British subjects. An additional article reaffirmed the condemnation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, and an act confirmed the perpetual neutrality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. The treaty also required that artworks looted by French armies since 1793 be returned, one of the first treaties to require the return of war booty on a large scale. The Quadruple Alliance was renewed in a separate treaty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The treaty was signed for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> by Viscount Castlereagh and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-wellesley-1st-duke-of-wellington">Duke of Wellington</a> and for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armand-emmanuel-du-plessis-duc-de-richelieu">Duc de Richelieu</a>. Wellington commanded the occupation, which was judged necessary for only three years, and the foreign troops left France in 1818 after the Congress of Aix-la-Chapelle. With interest and the upkeep of the army, the burden on France approached 1.7 billion francs. The renewed alliance introduced the idea of a peacetime congress to maintain peace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, on the pattern of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Paris (1815)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Napoleon's fall, the <b>Treaty of Paris</b> in November 1815 made the Ionian Islands, formerly the French Ionian Islands and earlier the Septinsular Republic, a nominally independent state under the exclusive protection of Britain.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-states-of-the-ionian-islands">United States of the Ionian Islands</a></li></ul></section>
